statement:
`board` is an `m x n` Minesweeper grid of one-character strings. A cell's neighbours are the up to eight cells around it, diagonals included. The symbols are:

- `'M'`: a mine that is still covered; `'E'`: a covered cell with no mine;
- `'B'`: an uncovered cell none of whose neighbours is a mine;
- `'1'` to `'8'`: an uncovered cell, showing how many of its neighbours are mines;
- `'X'`: an uncovered mine.

`click = [row, col]` names a covered cell (`'M'` or `'E'`) that the player opens. Update the board by these rules and return it once nothing else opens:

1. Opening a mine `'M'` turns it into `'X'`; the game ends and nothing else changes.
2. Opening an `'E'` with at least one neighbouring mine turns it into the digit giving that count, and opening goes no further from it.
3. Opening an `'E'` with no neighbouring mine turns it into `'B'`, and then each of its covered neighbours is opened too, by these same rules.

validator:
function validate([board, click]) {
  if (!Array.isArray(board) || board.length < 1 || board.length > 50) return 'board needs 1 to 50 rows.';
  const n = Array.isArray(board[0]) ? board[0].length : 0;
  if (n < 1 || n > 50) return 'Each row needs 1 to 50 cells.';
  for (const line of board) {
    if (!Array.isArray(line) || line.length !== n) return 'Every row must have the same length.';
    if (line.some(v => typeof v !== 'string' || !/^[MEB1-8]$/.test(v))) return "Cells must be 'M', 'E', 'B' or a digit 1-8.";
  }
  if (!Array.isArray(click) || click.length !== 2) return 'click must be [row, col].';
  const [r, c] = click;
  if (!Number.isInteger(r) || !Number.isInteger(c) || r < 0 || r >= board.length || c < 0 || c >= n) return 'click must be inside the board.';
  if (board[r][c] !== 'M' && board[r][c] !== 'E') return "The clicked cell must be 'M' or 'E'.";
  return null;
}

